---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import EncryptDecryptText from '@/components/tools/security/EncryptDecryptText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="encrypt-decrypt-text">
  <EncryptDecryptText client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to encrypt text with a password</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Paste the text into the <strong>Encrypt</strong> tab: recovery codes, a private note, a
        credential for a colleague, or any text you do not want to send in the clear.
      </li>
      <li>
        Choose a strong password and type it twice. The confirmation field is there because a typo here
        cannot be recovered. A passphrase of five or six random words is a good choice.
      </li>
      <li>
        Press <strong>Encrypt</strong>. The short pause is the key derivation: 200,000 rounds of PBKDF2,
        which an attacker must repeat for each guess.
      </li>
      <li>
        Copy the base64 block. It contains all data needed to decrypt except the password, so you can
        send it safely by email, chat or a text file.
      </li>
      <li>
        Send the password through a different channel, never in the same message: a phone call, a
        different app, or the secure-share feature of a password manager.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      To decrypt, open the <strong>Decrypt</strong> tab, paste the block and enter the password. The tool
      reads the envelope header when you paste, before you enter a password, so you see immediately if
      the text is a valid envelope and how many iterations it uses.
    </p>

    <h2>What actually happens to your text</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Step</th>
          <th>Algorithm</th>
          <th>Detail</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Salt generation</td>
          <td>crypto.getRandomValues</td>
          <td>16 new random bytes per message.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Key derivation</td>
          <td>PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256</td>
          <td>200,000 iterations, producing a 256-bit key.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Nonce generation</td>
          <td>crypto.getRandomValues</td>
          <td>12 new random bytes, the size specified for AES-GCM.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Encryption</td>
          <td>AES-256-GCM</td>
          <td>Authenticated encryption: confidentiality plus a 128-bit integrity tag.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Packaging</td>
          <td>Base64</td>
          <td>Magic, iteration count, salt, nonce and ciphertext in one printable block.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      All these primitives use the browser's own <code>crypto.subtle</code> implementation: native,
      audited code, not AES rewritten in JavaScript. The page publishes the exact byte layout of the
      envelope, so you can verify the format and decrypt it with any other AES-GCM implementation if this
      page disappears.
    </p>

    <h3>Why a fresh salt and nonce every time</h3>
    <p>
      The salt makes the derived key different for each message, even with the same password. This
      defeats precomputed tables, and an attacker who cracks one message does not get the others. The
      nonce is even more important: if a nonce is reused with the same key, AES-GCM fails completely, and
      an attacker can recover the authentication key and forge messages. Both come new from the CSPRNG for
      each encryption, so the same text encrypted twice gives two completely different outputs.
    </p>

    <h3>Why 200,000 iterations</h3>
    <p>
      PBKDF2 makes each password guess expensive. Without it, a password is hashed once and an attacker
      tests billions of candidates per second. With 200,000 iterations of HMAC-SHA256, the same hardware
      tests a few tens of thousands per second, about a million times slower. You also pay this cost, but
      only once and for a fraction of a second. The envelope stores the iteration count, so a future
      version can raise it without breaking messages encrypted today.
    </p>

    <h2>What this tool does not protect against</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A weak password. AES-256 cannot be broken in practice, but a password from a wordlist can. The key
        comes from the password, so the password sets the limit. For this reason the tool shows a live
        strength estimate.
      </li>
      <li>
        A lost password. There is no reset, recovery key or back door. Without the password, the text is
        lost permanently, by design.
      </li>
      <li>
        Metadata. Anyone who intercepts the message learns its approximate length and that it uses this
        format. The content is hidden; the fact that a message exists is not.
      </li>
      <li>
        A compromised device. If your machine has a keylogger, encryption on that machine does not help.
      </li>
      <li>
        Key exchange. You must still get the password to the other person. This is the hard part of
        cryptography, and this tool does not solve it; public-key systems exist for that.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When to use this, and when to use something else</h2>
    <p>
      Use this tool for a one-off secret that goes through a channel you do not fully trust: recovery codes
      in a note-taking app, a temporary credential emailed to a colleague, a small private record in a
      synced file. It is quick, needs no installation, and the output is plain text you can put anywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use other tools for ongoing key management, signatures that prove who sent a message, or protection
      of whole files and directories: an end-to-end encrypted messenger for conversations, public-key file
      encryption for files, an encrypted archive or a full-disk volume for bulk data, and a password
      manager for credentials you use often.
    </p>

    <h3>Understanding "decryption failed"</h3>
    <p>
      The tool cannot tell you that the password is wrong. It can tell you only that the authentication tag
      did not verify, which happens when the key is wrong and also when even one bit of the ciphertext has
      changed. This is a security property: an attacker cannot change your message and make it decrypt to
      something plausible. First check for a stray character from copy and paste, then check the password.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
